<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bernhard of Saxe-Weimar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1604–1639</span> · Also known as Bernard of Saxe-Weimar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bernhard of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> was a German prince and one of the leading Protestant generals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years' War. Serving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, he took command at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lutzen-1632">Lützen</a> in 1632 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> was killed, but was heavily defeated at Nördlingen in 1634. He then fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, captured the fortress of Breisach in 1638 and hoped to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>, but died suddenly in 1639.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bernhard-of-saxe-weimar.jpg" alt="Bernhard of Saxe-Weimar" width="320" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rijksmuseum · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portret_van_Bernhard,_hertog_van_Saksen-Weimar_Theatrum_pontificum,_imperatorum,_regum,_ducum_(serietitel),_RP-P-1911-5117.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
